{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "\n\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at this problem, I need to think about when collisions occur.\n\nKey observations:\n1. There are `n` cars moving left to right\n2. There are `n` cars moving right to left\n3. All cars move at the same speed\n4. Cars pass through each other (they're infinitely sturdy)\n5. The two sets start very far apart, so all left-to-right cars will eventually meet all right-to-left cars\n\nSince:\n- Every car moving left to right will eventually meet every car moving right to left (they're on a collision course)\n- Cars continue through each other after collision\n- All cars move at the same speed\n\nEach of the `n` left-to-right cars will collide with each of the `n` right-to-left cars exactly once.\n\nTherefore, the total number of collisions is `n \u00d7 n = n\u00b2`.\n\n```python\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\n    return n * n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "\n\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    seen = set()\n    for num in l:\n        if -num in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(num)\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "\n\ndef change_base(x: int, base: int):\n    \"\"\"Change numerical base of input number x to base.\n    return string representation after the conversion.\n    base numbers are less than 10.\n    >>> change_base(8, 3)\n    '22'\n    >>> change_base(8, 2)\n    '1000'\n    >>> change_base(7, 2)\n    '111'\n    \"\"\"\nHere's the code continuation for the `change_base` function:\n\n```python\n    if x == 0:\n        return '0'\n    \n    result = ''\n    while x > 0:\n        result = str(x % base) + result\n        x //= base\n    \n    return result\n```\n\nThe function works by:\n1. Handling the special case where x is 0\n2. Repeatedly dividing x by the base and prepending the remainder to the result string\n3. Continuing until x becomes 0\n4. Returning the accumulated string representation in the target base"}
